WEBVTT

00:00:00.550 --> 00:00:03.225
Welcome to the On-Chain Operator Program.

00:00:03.225 --> 00:00:04.820
I'm really glad you're here.

00:00:04.820 --> 00:00:06.994
Over the next ten minutes, I'll walk

00:00:06.994 --> 00:00:09.344
you through the whole program, stage by

00:00:09.344 --> 00:00:12.170
stage, with real examples from the lessons.

00:00:12.170 --> 00:00:14.971
By the end, you'll know exactly what you're building,

00:00:14.971 --> 00:00:17.305
how every lesson works, and what to do today.

00:00:18.605 --> 00:00:20.190
Here's where you'll end up.

00:00:20.190 --> 00:00:21.955
First, set up safely.

00:00:21.955 --> 00:00:24.745
Your accounts, wallets and backups, done right, so

00:00:24.745 --> 00:00:27.015
a single mistake can't wipe you out.

00:00:27.015 --> 00:00:29.010
Second, judge any position.

00:00:29.010 --> 00:00:30.794
What it pays you, what it risks,

00:00:30.794 --> 00:00:32.715
and exactly how you'd get out.

00:00:32.715 --> 00:00:34.580
And third, run your own bank.

00:00:34.580 --> 00:00:37.635
A balance sheet, custody rules, a credit line, and

00:00:37.635 --> 00:00:40.270
an income policy you can actually live with.

00:00:41.570 --> 00:00:44.880
Everything in this program comes back to one question.

00:00:44.880 --> 00:00:47.533
Most people see a number, like 12%

00:00:47.533 --> 00:00:50.485
APY, and ask how much they'll make.

00:00:50.485 --> 00:00:52.575
Operators ask something different.

00:00:52.575 --> 00:00:54.120
What am I being paid to risk?

00:00:55.420 --> 00:00:57.395
Here's that question in action.

00:00:57.395 --> 00:00:58.495
Two pools.

00:00:58.495 --> 00:01:00.435
Pool A shows 20%.

00:01:00.435 --> 00:01:03.290
But only 4% comes from trading fees.

00:01:03.290 --> 00:01:06.551
The other 16% is new tokens being printed as

00:01:06.551 --> 00:01:10.040
rewards, and those tokens can fall in price.

00:01:10.040 --> 00:01:12.024
Pool B shows 9%, and 8%

00:01:12.024 --> 00:01:13.895
of it is real fees.

00:01:13.895 --> 00:01:16.310
The bigger number isn't the better deal.

00:01:16.310 --> 00:01:18.049
You'll learn to split every yield

00:01:18.049 --> 00:01:19.175
like this in Module 4.

00:01:20.475 --> 00:01:21.690
Now the path.

00:01:21.690 --> 00:01:23.943
The program runs in six stages, and

00:01:23.943 --> 00:01:25.995
each one builds on the last.

00:01:25.995 --> 00:01:28.857
You'll unlock each stage by passing the one before,

00:01:28.857 --> 00:01:32.350
so you never reach leverage before you've mastered safety.

00:01:32.350 --> 00:01:34.953
Let's walk through them, with an example from each.

00:01:36.253 --> 00:01:38.723
Stage 0 assumes you know nothing.

00:01:38.723 --> 00:01:41.122
You'll secure your email, open and lock

00:01:41.122 --> 00:01:43.460
down an exchange account, make a first

00:01:43.460 --> 00:01:45.739
small purchase, set up a wallet, back

00:01:45.739 --> 00:01:48.198
it up, and send your first transfer.

00:01:48.198 --> 00:01:50.064
Then you'll practise on a free test

00:01:50.064 --> 00:01:52.138
network, where nothing real is at stake.

00:01:53.438 --> 00:01:55.583
Here's a Stage 0 example.

00:01:55.583 --> 00:01:56.843
The price on the chart

00:01:56.843 --> 00:01:58.238
is not what you pay.

00:01:58.238 --> 00:01:59.968
You lose a little to the spread, a

00:01:59.968 --> 00:02:01.945
little to the trading fee, and a little

00:02:01.945 --> 00:02:04.123
more to the network when you withdraw.

00:02:04.123 --> 00:02:06.785
So you'll compare offers by what actually lands

00:02:06.785 --> 00:02:09.115
in your wallet, not by the headline price.

00:02:10.415 --> 00:02:13.840
And Stage 0 ends with a security baseline.

00:02:13.840 --> 00:02:15.275
A hardware wallet.

00:02:15.275 --> 00:02:17.410
Unique passwords with two-factor.

00:02:17.410 --> 00:02:19.025
Never sharing your keys.

00:02:19.025 --> 00:02:20.528
And bookmarking the apps you

00:02:20.528 --> 00:02:22.435
use, instead of clicking links.

00:02:22.435 --> 00:02:24.450
These four habits prevent most of

00:02:24.450 --> 00:02:26.525
the losses people suffer in crypto.

00:02:27.825 --> 00:02:29.890
Stage 1 is foundations.

00:02:29.890 --> 00:02:32.194
You'll learn how transactions really work,

00:02:32.194 --> 00:02:33.995
and how to protect a wallet.

00:02:33.995 --> 00:02:37.455
One idea you'll use forever is the three-wallet setup.

00:02:37.455 --> 00:02:39.247
A vault for long-term holdings

00:02:39.247 --> 00:02:41.455
that almost never signs anything.

00:02:41.455 --> 00:02:44.160
An operator wallet for active positions.

00:02:44.160 --> 00:02:46.155
And a burner, for anything new.

00:02:46.155 --> 00:02:47.610
If a burner gets drained,

00:02:47.610 --> 00:02:49.010
you lose only the burner.

00:02:50.310 --> 00:02:52.275
Here's a Stage one example.

00:02:52.275 --> 00:02:54.066
Every time an app asks to move

00:02:54.066 --> 00:02:56.735
your tokens, it's asking for an approval.

00:02:56.735 --> 00:02:58.980
You'll read three things before you sign.

00:02:58.980 --> 00:03:00.155
Which token.

00:03:00.155 --> 00:03:01.920
Which contract can spend it.

00:03:01.920 --> 00:03:03.105
And how much.

00:03:03.105 --> 00:03:05.054
An infinite approval stays open

00:03:05.054 --> 00:03:06.815
after the trade is done.

00:03:06.815 --> 00:03:09.250
An exact one covers only this trade.

00:03:09.250 --> 00:03:11.226
You'll default to exact, and revoke

00:03:11.226 --> 00:03:12.489
what you no longer use.

00:03:13.789 --> 00:03:15.515
And here's one of the most common

00:03:15.515 --> 00:03:17.744
ways people lose money when they swap.

00:03:17.744 --> 00:03:20.157
A bot sees your trade waiting, buys just

00:03:20.157 --> 00:03:22.634
before you, and sells just after you.

00:03:22.634 --> 00:03:23.755
You get a worse price,

00:03:23.755 --> 00:03:25.419
and it keeps the difference.

00:03:25.419 --> 00:03:27.312
You'll learn how to shut that door:

00:03:27.312 --> 00:03:29.574
tight slippage, and a protected connection.

00:03:30.874 --> 00:03:33.595
And here's a Stage 1 example with numbers.

00:03:33.595 --> 00:03:36.595
When you provide liquidity to a 50/50 pool

00:03:36.595 --> 00:03:39.385
and the price doubles, you end up about

00:03:39.385 --> 00:03:42.545
5.7% worse off than if you'd simply held.

00:03:42.545 --> 00:03:45.820
If it halves, it's the same 5.7%.

00:03:45.820 --> 00:03:48.320
If it triples, it's 13.4%.

00:03:48.320 --> 00:03:50.475
Your trading fees have to beat that line,

00:03:50.475 --> 00:03:52.425
or you'd have been better off holding.

00:03:53.725 --> 00:03:56.545
Stage two is where you become a practitioner.

00:03:56.545 --> 00:03:59.281
Lending, borrowing, yield and staking, and

00:03:59.281 --> 00:04:02.445
the infrastructure every position depends on.

00:04:02.445 --> 00:04:04.780
You'll start with how a lending pool works.

00:04:04.780 --> 00:04:08.276
Suppliers deposit, borrowers pay interest, and when a

00:04:08.276 --> 00:04:11.319
pool is nearly fully borrowed, rates jump, and

00:04:11.319 --> 00:04:13.650
lenders may not be able to withdraw.

00:04:14.950 --> 00:04:16.743
And here's a worked example

00:04:16.743 --> 00:04:18.835
you'll master in Lesson 3.2.

00:04:18.835 --> 00:04:21.241
You deposit 10 ETH at

00:04:21.241 --> 00:04:25.150
$3,000, so $30,000 of collateral.

00:04:25.150 --> 00:04:26.985
You borrow $12,000.

00:04:26.985 --> 00:04:32.260
The health factor is $30,000 × 0.8 ÷ $12,000.

00:04:32.260 --> 00:04:33.305
That's 2.

00:04:33.305 --> 00:04:37.150
The liquidation price is $12,000 ÷ 8.

00:04:37.150 --> 00:04:39.005
$1,500.

00:04:39.005 --> 00:04:43.090
So ETH can fall 50% before you're liquidated.

00:04:44.390 --> 00:04:46.685
Here's the same position as a chart.

00:04:46.685 --> 00:04:49.022
The blue line is your health factor

00:04:49.022 --> 00:04:51.010
as the price of ETH moves.

00:04:51.010 --> 00:04:54.105
Borrow more, and you get the orange line instead.

00:04:54.105 --> 00:04:58.135
It hits liquidation at $2,000, not $1,500.

00:04:58.135 --> 00:04:59.715
More debt moves the cliff edge

00:04:59.715 --> 00:05:01.645
closer to where you're standing.

00:05:01.645 --> 00:05:04.690
You'll never borrow again without drawing this line first.

00:05:05.990 --> 00:05:08.235
Stage three makes you an analyst.

00:05:08.235 --> 00:05:11.300
You'll research any protocol with a six-step loop.

00:05:11.300 --> 00:05:12.525
How it works.

00:05:12.525 --> 00:05:14.130
Where its cash comes from.

00:05:14.130 --> 00:05:15.505
What it depends on.

00:05:15.505 --> 00:05:16.930
Whether it's solvent.

00:05:16.930 --> 00:05:18.445
What the evidence says.

00:05:18.445 --> 00:05:19.920
And how you'd get out.

00:05:19.920 --> 00:05:22.092
You'll finish the stage with a real

00:05:22.092 --> 00:05:24.385
due-diligence file on a real protocol.

00:05:25.685 --> 00:05:27.143
You'll also learn to read

00:05:27.143 --> 00:05:29.630
on-chain data without fooling yourself.

00:05:29.630 --> 00:05:32.585
Volume can be faked by trading back and forth.

00:05:32.585 --> 00:05:35.345
A thousand addresses might be one person.

00:05:35.345 --> 00:05:37.407
And total value locked counts money

00:05:37.407 --> 00:05:39.655
that's only there for the rewards.

00:05:39.655 --> 00:05:41.436
Every metric gets its caveat, and

00:05:41.436 --> 00:05:42.745
you'll learn to check it.

00:05:44.045 --> 00:05:46.670
Stage four is the strategy library.

00:05:46.670 --> 00:05:50.301
Thirty strategies in seven levels, from simple stablecoin

00:05:50.301 --> 00:05:53.745
lending, through liquidity and yield, up to fixed-rate

00:05:53.745 --> 00:05:57.175
yield, basis trades, options and hedged positions.

00:05:57.175 --> 00:05:59.656
Every one comes with its maths, its kill

00:05:59.656 --> 00:06:01.955
rules, and exactly how it loses money.

00:06:03.255 --> 00:06:05.770
Here's how a strategist thinks about yield.

00:06:05.770 --> 00:06:08.075
Take a farm paying 18%.

00:06:08.075 --> 00:06:10.698
Suppose there's a 10% chance each year

00:06:10.698 --> 00:06:12.581
of a loss, and a loss would

00:06:12.581 --> 00:06:14.360
cost 60% of the money.

00:06:14.360 --> 00:06:16.875
That's an expected loss of 6%.

00:06:16.875 --> 00:06:19.880
So the expected yield is 12, not 18.

00:06:19.880 --> 00:06:21.705
And the 40% farm?

00:06:21.705 --> 00:06:24.255
After its expected loss, it barely beats

00:06:24.255 --> 00:06:26.370
the safer one, with far more risk.

00:06:27.670 --> 00:06:29.242
And stage five is where you

00:06:29.242 --> 00:06:30.945
operate as your own bank.

00:06:30.945 --> 00:06:33.015
It's a method, not a licence.

00:06:33.015 --> 00:06:35.704
You'll build a balance sheet, set custody rules,

00:06:35.704 --> 00:06:38.119
open a credit line against your own assets,

00:06:38.119 --> 00:06:40.863
and ladder your liquidity, so you're never forced

00:06:40.863 --> 00:06:42.400
to sell at the worst moment.

00:06:43.700 --> 00:06:45.935
Here's the custody design you'll build.

00:06:45.935 --> 00:06:47.780
Most of your money sits in a vault

00:06:47.780 --> 00:06:50.030
that needs more than one key to move.

00:06:50.030 --> 00:06:53.740
Active positions live in an operating wallet with limits.

00:06:53.740 --> 00:06:56.355
And a small hot float handles anything new.

00:06:56.355 --> 00:06:58.739
No single lost phone, stolen key or

00:06:58.739 --> 00:07:00.925
bad signature can drain the bank.

00:07:02.225 --> 00:07:05.400
Here's the liquidity ladder, using an example

00:07:05.400 --> 00:07:07.770
of $5,000 a month of spending.

00:07:07.770 --> 00:07:11.330
One month sits instantly available, in stablecoins.

00:07:11.330 --> 00:07:13.804
Five more months sit in blue-chip lending

00:07:13.804 --> 00:07:15.695
you can reach the same day.

00:07:15.695 --> 00:07:17.499
The next 6 to 12 months are in

00:07:17.499 --> 00:07:21.000
fixed-term positions that mature when you'll need them.

00:07:21.000 --> 00:07:22.645
Everything else can grow.

00:07:22.645 --> 00:07:24.733
You refill each rung on a schedule,

00:07:24.733 --> 00:07:26.764
so you never sell growth assets in

00:07:26.764 --> 00:07:28.330
a bad market to pay a bill.

00:07:29.630 --> 00:07:31.775
And finally, the income engine.

00:07:31.775 --> 00:07:33.770
You start with the headline yield.

00:07:33.770 --> 00:07:36.005
You subtract the losses you expect.

00:07:36.005 --> 00:07:38.270
You keep part of what's left as a buffer.

00:07:38.270 --> 00:07:39.886
And only then do you decide

00:07:39.886 --> 00:07:41.455
what you can take out.

00:07:41.455 --> 00:07:43.400
You never pay out from principal.

00:07:43.400 --> 00:07:46.040
This is an illustration, not a promise.

00:07:46.040 --> 00:07:49.631
Nothing in this program promises income, and you'll learn

00:07:49.631 --> 00:07:52.665
why that honesty is what keeps a portfolio alive.

00:07:53.965 --> 00:07:56.030
Now, how every lesson works.

00:07:56.030 --> 00:07:57.350
Watch the video.

00:07:57.350 --> 00:07:59.741
It walks through the idea and the numbers

00:07:59.741 --> 00:08:02.660
on screen, just like the examples you've seen.

00:08:02.660 --> 00:08:04.515
Then do the checklist, for real,

00:08:04.515 --> 00:08:06.220
with a small test amount.

00:08:06.220 --> 00:08:08.186
Then check yourself with three quiz

00:08:08.186 --> 00:08:10.070
questions before you move on.

00:08:10.070 --> 00:08:12.075
The checklist is where the learning sticks.

00:08:13.375 --> 00:08:16.390
Let's try one now, the way every lesson ends.

00:08:16.390 --> 00:08:18.335
A pool pays 20%.

00:08:18.335 --> 00:08:21.870
4% is fees, and 16% is emissions.

00:08:21.870 --> 00:08:23.365
Which part is more durable?

00:08:27.785 --> 00:08:30.267
The answer: the 4% from fees.

00:08:30.267 --> 00:08:32.707
Emissions are paid in a token that can fall.

00:08:34.007 --> 00:08:35.879
And if a topic is brand new to

00:08:35.879 --> 00:08:39.017
you, every module opens with a Mastery Starter.

00:08:39.017 --> 00:08:41.353
It takes about ten minutes, and gets you

00:08:41.353 --> 00:08:43.647
from zero to ready for that module.

00:08:43.647 --> 00:08:45.620
The words you'll need, what to have in

00:08:45.620 --> 00:08:47.492
place first, and one safe first step.

00:08:48.792 --> 00:08:50.397
You'll also have tools.

00:08:50.397 --> 00:08:53.720
Calculators for the health factor, impermanent loss,

00:08:53.720 --> 00:08:56.480
leveraged loops and income, so every number

00:08:56.480 --> 00:08:59.037
you act on is computed, not guessed.

00:08:59.037 --> 00:09:01.347
Worksheets for due diligence, pre-launch

00:09:01.347 --> 00:09:03.337
checks and your bank policies.

00:09:03.337 --> 00:09:05.667
The Course Hub, with search, your progress,

00:09:05.667 --> 00:09:07.502
and every video in one place.

00:09:07.502 --> 00:09:09.414
And two capstones, analyst and

00:09:09.414 --> 00:09:11.697
operator, that lead to certification.

00:09:12.997 --> 00:09:14.752
Three rules, from day one.

00:09:14.752 --> 00:09:17.112
Never share your seed phrase or keys.

00:09:17.112 --> 00:09:19.642
Nobody from this program will ever ask for

00:09:19.642 --> 00:09:22.237
them, and anyone who does is a scammer.

00:09:22.237 --> 00:09:24.442
Use small test amounts while you learn,

00:09:24.442 --> 00:09:26.562
only what you can afford to lose.

00:09:26.562 --> 00:09:27.913
And write the exit down

00:09:27.913 --> 00:09:29.602
before you enter any position.

00:09:30.902 --> 00:09:32.522
So here's your first day.

00:09:32.522 --> 00:09:36.102
Open Module 0, and print the Day-1 Setup Kit.

00:09:36.102 --> 00:09:38.198
Secure your email with a password

00:09:38.198 --> 00:09:40.840
manager and 2FA, before anything else.

00:09:40.840 --> 00:09:43.120
Work through Part A of the kit, in order.

00:09:43.120 --> 00:09:44.385
There's no rush.

00:09:44.385 --> 00:09:46.437
Then take the Lesson 0.1 quiz,

00:09:46.437 --> 00:09:47.960
and you're on your way.

00:09:49.260 --> 00:09:50.560
One last thing.

00:09:50.560 --> 00:09:53.312
This is education, not financial advice,

00:09:53.312 --> 00:09:55.625
and no results are guaranteed.

00:09:55.625 --> 00:09:57.590
What you're getting is a process.

00:09:57.590 --> 00:10:00.253
One you can explain, position by position,

00:10:00.253 --> 00:10:01.863
for as long as you invest.

00:10:03.163 --> 00:10:05.138
Welcome aboard, operator.

00:10:05.138 --> 00:10:07.473
Open Module 0, and let's begin.
